WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.389 --> 00:00:05.259
How much pain can the human body actually
handle?

00:00:05.259 --> 00:00:08.410
And can pain become so intense that it causes
death?

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:09.410
Can pain actually kill?

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:15.639
Let's find out.

00:00:15.639 --> 00:00:19.070
First we need to understand how pain actually
works.

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:21.400
There are four basic steps to feeling pain.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:23.510
The first is contact with the stimulus.

00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:26.050
This is the thing which actually causes the
pain.

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:30.769
For example, a knife cutting through your
finger or fire burning your skin.

00:00:30.769 --> 00:00:34.570
Secondly is the reception of the pain by your
nerve endings.

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:36.390
Nerves cover every inch of your body.

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:41.780
As soon as a nerve ending detects tissue damage
or potential tissue damage, it alerts your

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:46.790
brain by sending a pain signal, so you can
hopefully stop whatever it is that is causing

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:48.340
that tissue damage.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:51.469
This takes us on to the third step, transmission.

00:00:51.469 --> 00:00:55.670
The nerve sends a signal to the central nervous
system in your brain.

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:58.180
Which is made up of neurons.

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:01.329
The fourth and final step is processing.

00:01:01.329 --> 00:01:04.600
Your central nervous system has been informed
of the pain signal.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:08.830
The neurons in your brain now communicate
with each other to determine if the threat

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:14.530
is great enough to warrant a pain response,
and if so, your brain calculates how much

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:16.070
pain is required.

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:19.670
Your brain then acts on the decision, and
you feel pain.

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:23.510
But is there a cap to how much pain we can
feel?

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:29.310
Well yes, since our brain decides when and
how much pain we can feel, it can also shut

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:30.450
it off.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:35.229
Usually it does this by shutting itself down
completely when the central nervous system

00:01:35.229 --> 00:01:37.040
becomes overloaded.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:42.159
Subjects of torture often report passing out
when the level of pain becomes to great to

00:01:42.159 --> 00:01:43.290
handle.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:48.140
Before modern medicine and the invention of
anaesthetic, it was extremely commonplace

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:53.380
and considered quite normal for patients to
pass out whilst undergoing an amputation.

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:58.159
But the level of pain required to pass out
can't be measured as an exact figure, because

00:01:58.159 --> 00:02:02.060
pain is subjective, and everyone feels pain
differently.

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:08.590
In the 1940s James D. Hardy tried to introduce
a standardised measurement of pain levels,

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:09.750
called the Dolorimeter.

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:15.810
But the idea never caught on because everyone
experiences pain in a unique way, so the measurements

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:17.300
were highly inconsistent.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:20.160
But can pain kill you?

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:24.250
Well turns out it can, although somewhat indirectly.

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:28.930
You can't die from receiving too many pain
signals, because your brain will shut down

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:33.260
and you will pass out before it does any serious
damage to your brain cells.

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:35.980
You can however die from shock.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:39.010
No I'm not talking about emotional shock.

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:45.050
There is a separate medical condition caused
circulatory shock, which is caused by an acute,

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:46.860
intense level of pain.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:52.340
When your body goes into shock insufficient
blood and oxygen reaches your cells, resulting

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:54.849
in rapid and permanent tissue damage.

00:02:54.849 --> 00:03:00.010
If shock is not treated quickly it can cause
fatal damage to your organs and your brain.

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:04.520
And in some cases this can result in a rather
quick death.

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:09.450
In fact during wars, medics found that administering
pain killers such as morphine to a wounded

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:15.349
soldier and therefore preventing them from
going into shock could often save their life.

00:03:15.349 --> 00:03:19.450
They found that the quicker the morphine was
administered, the higher the survival rate

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:21.430
of wounded soldiers.

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:26.849
Shock and pain was such a lethality, that
during World War II, soldiers carried morphine

00:03:26.849 --> 00:03:28.760
with them on the battlefield.

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:34.299
As it could often save their lives by massively
reducing pain levels after sustaining a serious

00:03:34.299 --> 00:03:35.489
injury.

00:03:35.489 --> 00:03:40.129
So yes pain can kill you.

